  <steps mode="directional">
    <step order="0">Declare rigor FULL. Confirm 144 (including its dev root migration), 145, 146, 149 merged; read the child census and lookup sets task 146 recorded.</step>
    <step order="1">Build `SecureChildReconciler.ReconcileAsync(rootType, rootId, mode)`: enumerate children through direct lookups, stamps, and one further hop through document/invoice/communication/event hosts; for each, ask the resolver for its owner; when the target is the Secure team, first mirror the root's sharee access onto the child (149's mechanism), then assign if different (read-back); when leaving secure, assign first, then remove the mirrored shares; finish with 149's reconcile(root) — the ordering constraint governs. Return a per-table report (examined / already correct / changed / refused / failed) with previous owners.</step>
    <step order="2">Wire provisioning (all three root types) and unsecure in the ordering the constraint fixes; add counts and reason codes to the responses; make re-invocation on an incomplete child pass resume it.</step>
    <step order="3">Add `SecureChildReconciliationJob` (disabled, report-only by default) sweeping every secure root; per-run cap; progress log.</step>
    <step order="4">Tests: provisioning re-owns existing children and grandchildren and mirrors shares; unsecure returns children to the non-secure owner and removes mirrored shares before revoking the root's; a failed child pass reports counts and (unsecure) keeps the flag; second run changes nothing; report-only writes nothing; excluded rows (unfiled documents, per-user rows, secure-flagged child roots) untouched.</step>
    <step order="5">Operator runbook in the guide; I-2 row update. Build, tests, publish size, CVE.</step>
    <step order="6" mode="prescriptive">MANUAL LIVE GATE (dev, after deploy): seed probe children (document via `sprk_project`, document via `sprk_relatedproject`, event, to-do regarding the document, communication, memo) under an ordinary project, matter and work assignment owned by an existing non-admin test user; provision each secure; record owner read-back + share mirror + that a non-sharee test user is denied; unsecure one of them and record the reverse; run the sweep report-only over all secure roots and record the plan; run it with writes on for dev's existing secure roots; run it again and record zero changes; delete probes and record the deletion.</step>
    <step order="7">Update TASK-INDEX.md (`✅ [done]`) and the POML status; write the task note (reports attached).</step>
  </steps>

  <escalation>
    <trigger>The dev report-only sweep finds existing children of a secure root whose CONTENT sits in a shared SPE container (documents created before the root's own container): re-owning the Dataverse row does not move bytes already in shared storage. Report rows and locations and ask before applying. RECOMMENDATION: apply the row re-own anyway (it strictly reduces exposure and does not touch bytes), and present moving the content into the root's own container as an owner decision to be added to THIS task's scope (owner: nothing deferred) — cross-reference task 144's identical trigger for secure roots.</trigger>
    <trigger>Re-owning a child is refused by Dataverse naming a privilege task 145 did not grant: STOP — the fix belongs in 145's codified set, not in a wider role here.</trigger>
    <trigger>A root→child relationship cascades Assign (task 149 Part A records it): the root re-own already moved some children; reconcile the two behaviours with the owner before applying the sweep.</trigger>
    <trigger>Task 133's (C11) provisioning change is being implemented concurrently and conflicts with this task's resume behaviour on ProvisionProjectEndpoint.cs: coordinate before editing; do not ship two resume paths.</trigger>
  </escalation>

  <justification>
    <existing>No component re-owns or re-shares existing children: grep `sprk_documents|sprk_events|sprk_todos` in `ProvisionProjectEndpoint.cs` and `UnsecureProjectEndpoint.cs` → none (both touch `sprk_projects` only, `:80` / `:46`). Nearest neighbours: `scripts/Backfill-CoreAncestorStamps.ps1` (stamps, not owners) and word-add-in-r1's `scripts/Backfill-RecordOwnership.ps1` (app-owned rows only, not on this branch, PowerShell reimplementation of the rule). Jobs: `ExternalAccessReconciliationJob.cs:94` (grant rows), `MembershipReconciliationJob.cs:146` (membership junction) — different invariants.</existing>
    <extension>No existing class owns "bring a root's children to their invariant state": extending the provisioning endpoint alone would leave unsecure and the backfill with a second copy, and neither reconciliation job reconciles ownership. One engine (`SecureChildReconciler`) called from three triggers is the write-path architecture's L1/L4 shape; the job is the ADR-036 form of the L4 trigger (task 147 reuses it as the safety net).</extension>
    <cost-of-doing-nothing>A project, matter or work assignment made secure after it has children leaves every one of them readable by ordinary users in MDA; an unsecured record leaves its children owned by a memberless team and reachable by nobody; and every record that is secure today keeps its children exposed permanently.</cost-of-doing-nothing>
  </justification>

